    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=\\dfrac{3\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}$.\n\nRewrite the left side as $\\big((x-1)^2-x\\big)^2$.",
      "worked": "Write $x^2-x+1=(x-1)^2+x$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}(x^2-x+1)^2-4x(x-1)^2&=\\big((x-1)^2+x\\big)^2-4x(x-1)^2\\\\(x^2-x+1)^2-4x(x-1)^2&=(x-1)^4-2x(x-1)^2+x^2\\\\(x^2-x+1)^2-4x(x-1)^2&=\\big((x-1)^2-x\\big)^2\\\\(x^2-x+1)^2-4x(x-1)^2&=(x^2-3x+1)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$\\begin{align*}(x^2-3x+1)^2&=0\\\\x^2-3x+1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{3\\pm\\sqrt{9-4}}2\\\\x&=\\frac{3\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe identity above verifies both roots in the original equation.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
